1.0	Introduction  

This document specifies the functional and non-functional requirements for the Authenticator mobile application, a secure offline Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) generator designed for two-factor authentication without requiring internet connectivity.

Purpose  

This System Requirements Specification (SRS) document provides a complete description of all requirements for the Authenticator Application. The intended audience for this document includes project managers, software developers, testers, and quality assurance personnel involved in the design, development, and validation of the software product.
Authenticator is a secure offline authenticator app designed to generate TOTP codes for multi-factor authentication without relying on internet access.

Scope 

This specification defines the requirements for a mobile Authenticator Application that generates Time-based One-Time Passwords (TOTP) for two-factor authentication. The software product will be developed as a cross-platform mobile application for iOS and Android devices.
All operations are performed offline with zero server interaction, ensuring maximum privacy and protection from data breaches.

The software product will:
1.	Enable users to manage multiple authentication accounts with search, filter, and favorites
2.	Generate TOTP codes compliant with RFC 6238
3.	Provide secure storage of cryptographic secrets using XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD encryption
4.	Support account registration via QR code scanning (otpauth:// and otpauth-migration://) and manual entry
5.	Create password-protected encrypted backups for cross-device recovery
6.	Import accounts from Google Authenticator and other compatible apps
7.	Export accounts via QR codes for device-to-device transfers
8.	Perform device security checks (root/jailbreak detection)
9.	Verify database integrity to detect tampering
10.	Support bulk operations (multi-select delete/export)
11.	Provide recovery codes for account recovery
12.	Display brand-specific icons for popular services
13.	Support dark/light themes
14.	Include onboarding for first-time users

Definitions, Acronyms, and Abbreviations.  

Term	Definition
2FA	Two-Factor Authentication - A security process where users provide two different authentication factors
TOTP	Time-based One-Time Password - A temporary passcode generated based on current time and a secret key
HOTP	HMAC-based One-Time Password - A counter-based one-time password algorithm
HMAC	Hash-based Message Authentication Code - A specific type of message authentication code
OTP	One-Time Password - A password that is valid for only one login session or transaction
QR Code	Quick Response Code - A type of matrix barcode used to store information
AES	Advanced Encryption Standard - Symmetric encryption algorithm
Keychain 	iOS secure storage system for sensitive
Keystore	Android secure storage system with hardware-backed encryption


References  

Reference	Description
RFC 6238	TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm
RFC 4226	HOTP: An HMAC-Based One-Time Password Algorithm
NIST SP 800-63B	Digital Identity Guidelines - Authentication and Lifecycle Management
Flutter Framework Documentation	Cross-platform mobile development framework
Google Authenticator PAM	otpauth URI format specification
 

Overview  
This document is organized into three main sections. 

Section 1 (Introduction) provides the purpose, scope, and background information. 

Section 2 (The Overall Description) describes the general factors that affect the product and its requirements from a user perspective. 

Section 3 (Specific Requirements) contains detailed, technical requirements written for software developers and testers. The document concludes with supporting information and risk assessment.

2.0	 The Overall Description  

Product Perspective  

The Authenticator Application is a self-contained mobile software component that operates within the broader context of two-factor authentication systems. It serves as a standalone token generator that works in conjunction with various online services that support TOTP-based 2FA. The application interfaces with the mobile device's operating system for secure storage, camera functionality, and user interaction, but requires no connection to proprietary backend systems for its core operation.

The system context can be represented as:



 

System Interfaces

The application shall interface with the host mobile operating system (iOS/Android) for:
1.	Secure storage services (Keychain/Keystore) for cryptographic key protection
2.	Camera services for QR code capture and processing
3.	Clipboard services for OTP transfer to other applications
4.	Biometric authentication services for application access control
User Interfaces

The application has one main interface, which is called the Account List Screen.
For new users, it shows an “Add Account” button for adding accounts via a QR code scan or manually using a bottom sheet.
Upon the addition of an account, the same screen shows a list of all accounts with an Account Name, Issuer Name, 6-digit OTP, and a countdown timer updating every second.
The OTP automatically refreshes every 30 seconds, while a floating “+” button stays available to add more accounts.
The overall UI is minimal, secure, and operates wholly offline to guarantee data privacy.

Figure 1 Initial Add Account Screen
Figure 2 Account List with OTPs
 

Hardware Interfaces
 
•	Camera: The application shall utilize the device's rear-facing camera for QR code scanning with automatic focus and exposure capabilities.
•	Secure Element: The application shall utilize hardware-backed secure storage (Android Keystore, iOS Secure Enclave) for cryptographic key protection.
•	Biometric Sensors: The application may utilize fingerprint readers or facial recognition systems for application locking.

Software Interfaces

•	Cryptographic Libraries: The application shall use platform-agnostic cryptographic libraries for TOTP computation implementing HMAC-SHA1.
•	Secure Storage APIs: The application shall interface with platform-specific secure storage APIs (Android Keystore, iOS Keychain).
•	Camera APIs: The application shall use platform camera APIs for image capture and processing.
•	Biometric APIs: The application shall use platform biometric authentication APIs when application locking is enabled.

 
Communications Interfaces

The application requires no network communication interfaces for its primary TOTP generation functionality. All operations are performed locally on the device.

Memory Constraints

The application targets modern mobile devices running iOS 12.0+ and Android 8.0+ (API level 26+), which typically have 2GB+ RAM and adequate storage capacity.
Memory usage will be optimized during development following Flutter performance best practices, but no specific memory limits are defined as system requirements.

Operations

The application supports the following operational modes:
•	Account Registration: Users can add new authentication accounts by scanning QR codes or manual entry.
•	OTP Generation: Continuous background generation and display of time-based one-time passwords.
•	Account Management: Users can view, organize, and remove authentication accounts.
•	Application Security: Optional application locking using device passcode or biometric authentication


Site Adaptation Requirements

No specific site adaptation requirements are necessary. The application is designed for standard consumer mobile devices and requires no special installation or configuration at deployment sites.

Product Functions 

The major functions provided by the software are:
1.	Account Registration
o	Users can quickly add new authentication accounts by scanning QR codes (otpauth:// or otpauth-migration://) presented by online services
o	Manual entry of account details including secret key, issuer, and account name
o	Validation of input parameters and secret key format
o	Bulk import from Google Authenticator exports
2.	TOTP Generation
o	Time-synchronized generation of 6-digit codes using HMAC-SHA1
o	Automatic code updates every 30 seconds
o	Support for multiple concurrent accounts
o	Real-time countdown indicators
3.	Secure Storage
o	All secret keys are encrypted using XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD before storage
o	Master encryption key stored in flutter_secure_storage with hardware-backed protection
o	SQLite database for structured account data
o	Database integrity verification using SHA256 checksums
4.	Backup & Restore
	o	Password-protected encrypted backups (.cdac files)
	o	Argon2id KDF for password-based encryption (64MB, 3 iterations, 4 parallelism)
	o	Backup includes accounts, favorites, and settings
	o	Merge strategies for handling duplicates (skip/replace/keep both)
	o	File sharing via system share sheet
5.	Import & Export
	o	Import from Google Authenticator via otpauth-migration:// QR codes
	o	Export accounts as QR codes using Google Authenticator format
	o	Device-to-device transfers without cloud
	o	Bulk account operations
6.	User Interface
	o	Clear display of accounts with current OTP values and brand icons
	o	Visual countdown indicators showing OTP validity period
	o	Search and filter by issuer or account name
	o	Favorites system for quick access
	o	Bulk selection for delete/export operations
	o	Swipe gestures for quick actions
	o	Dark/light theme support
	o	Onboarding for first-time users
7.	Security Features
o	Optional application locking mechanism (PIN/biometric)
o	Device security checks (root/jailbreak, emulator detection)
o	Secure clipboard handling for OTP transfer
o	Protection against screen capture and logging of sensitive data
o	Recovery codes for account recovery
o	Database tampering detection

 

User Characteristics 

The intended users include anyone who needs to enable two-factor authentication for their online accounts, regardless of technical background or education level.

The user interface is designed to be immediately intuitive - users simply scan a QR code from the service they're securing, and the app automatically begins generating time-based verification codes. No technical knowledge is required to operate the application successfully.

Constraints  

The app must work within these limits:
1.	Follow Google and Apple's app store rules
2.	Only work on phones that have a camera and secure storage
3.	Work with the QR codes that websites provide for setting up 2FA
4.	Keep all secret codes safe on users phone only

Assumptions and Dependencies

We're counting on these things being true:
1.	User phone's clock shows the correct time
2.	User phone keeps user data secure like it promises
3.	Websites give us the right kind of QR codes to scan

Apportioning of Requirements.

First Version:

QR code scanning to add accounts
Manual account entry
Device lock protection (PIN/pattern/biometric)
Encrypted backup and restore
Import from Google Authenticator
Export via QR code
Search and filter accounts
Bulk delete operations
Swipe gestures
Light and dark themes

