3.2	Functional Requirements

3.2.1 	Account Registration via QR Code
Input : QR code with otpauth:// URI or otpauth-migration:// URI (Google Authenticator export)
Process : Scan QR using mobile_scanner, parse URI, validate secret, encrypt secret, store account in database
Output : New account(s) saved, show success message with count
3.2.2 	Add Account Manually
Input : Issuer, account name, secret key (Base32)
Process : Validate Base32 secret, check duplicates, encrypt secret, store account in database
Output : New account saved, show errors if invalid
3.2.3 	Generate TOTP
Input : Current time, encrypted secrets from database
Process : Decrypt secrets, calculate time step, compute HMAC-SHA1, generate 6-digit code
Output : OTP codes for all accounts, update every 30s
3.2.4 	Display Accounts
Input : Stored account data from database
Process : Show account list with issuer, name, OTP, timer, brand icons
Output : Scrollable account list with live updates, search, filter, favorites
3.2.5 	Copy OTP
Input : User tap on OTP
Process : Copy code to clipboard, start 30s clear timer
Output : OTP in clipboard, show copied message
3.2.6 	Secure Storage
Input : Account data with secrets
Process : Encrypt secrets using XChaCha20-Poly1305, store in SQLite database
Output : Encrypted data stored securely with hardware-backed key
3.2.7 	Create Encrypted Backup
Input : User password (6+ characters)
Process : Collect all accounts, favorites, settings; derive key using Argon2id; encrypt with XChaCha20-Poly1305; save as .cdac file
Output : Encrypted backup file with timestamp, shareable via system share sheet
3.2.8 	Restore from Backup
Input : .cdac backup file, user password, merge strategy (skip/replace/keep both)
Process : Decrypt backup using Argon2id + XChaCha20-Poly1305; check for duplicates; apply merge strategy; import accounts
Output : Accounts restored, show summary (imported/skipped/replaced counts)
3.2.9 	Export Accounts via QR
Input : Selected accounts (single or multiple)
Process : Encode accounts in Google Authenticator protobuf format; generate otpauth-migration:// URI; create QR code
Output : QR code displayed on screen for scanning by another device
3.2.10 	Import from Google Authenticator
Input : otpauth-migration:// QR code
Process : Scan QR, decode base64 protobuf, parse accounts, check duplicates, import
Output : Multiple accounts imported, show success count
3.2.11 	Device Security Check
Input : Device state
Process : Check for jailbreak/root, emulator, development mode, external storage using safe_device
Output : Security warning if device is insecure (in production mode)
3.2.12 	Database Integrity Verification
Input : Database content
Process : Calculate SHA256 checksum, compare with baseline, update after legitimate changes
Output : Integrity status (pass/fail), log tampering attempts
3.2.13 	Search and Filter Accounts
Input : Search query, filter criteria
Process : Filter accounts by issuer or account name, show matching results
Output : Filtered account list
3.2.14 	Favorite Accounts
Input : User tap on star icon or swipe gesture
Process : Toggle favorite status, persist in shared preferences
Output : Favorites shown at top of list
3.2.15 	Bulk Operations
Input : Long-press to enter selection mode
Process : Multi-select accounts for delete or export
Output : Selected accounts deleted or exported
3.2.16 	Recovery Codes
Input : User request
Process : Generate 10 one-time recovery codes (8 chars, XXXX-XXXX format), hash with SHA256, store
Output : Recovery codes displayed once for user to save

3.3	Performance Requirements
 
No specific performance limits have been defined. The application shall be optimized for responsive user experience with typical account usage patterns.

3.4	Logical Database Requirements

Store : issuer, account name, encrypted secret, algorithm, digits, period
Constraints : Unique issuer+account names, valid Base32 secrets

3.5	Design Constraints

•	All crypto on device only
•	No network transmission of secrets
•	Works offline
•	Follow platform UI guidelines

3.6	Standards Compliance  

•	RFC 6238 for TOTP
•	otpauth URI scheme
•	Platform security APIs

3.7	Software System Attributes


3.7.1	Reliability

The system shall maintain data integrity with zero loss of account secrets. The application shall recover from crashes without data corruption.

3.7.2	Availability

The system shall be available for use whenever the mobile device is operational. Application restart after failure shall restore all account data and functionality immediately.

3.7.3	Security

•	Secrets are stored encrypted in SQLite database using XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD encryption.
•	Master encryption key stored in flutter_secure_storage with automatic hardware-backed protection (Android Keystore / iOS Keychain).
•	Password-based backups use Argon2id KDF (64MB memory, 3 iterations, 4 parallelism) + XChaCha20-Poly1305 for cross-device recovery.
•	No data leaves the device (no cloud, no network communication).
•	Secrets and OTPs are never logged or shared.
•	Clipboard auto-clears after 30 seconds.
•	Database integrity verification using SHA256 checksums to detect tampering.
•	Device security checks on app launch (root/jailbreak, emulator, development mode detection).
•	App Lock — The system shall provide an optional PIN (bcrypt-hashed) or biometric authentication layer to restrict unauthorized access to stored secrets and app UI.
•	Recovery codes (10 one-time codes, SHA256-hashed) for account recovery.
•	Screen capture prevention and privacy overlay on app switch.

3.7.4	Maintainability

The codebase shall be structured to allow modifications and feature additions by other developers.

3.7.5	Portability

The system shall maintain identical functionality across iOS and Android platforms from a single codebase.

3.7.6	Additional Comments
Functional requirements are organized by feature hierarchy. All requirements are testable and verifiable.


